---
import IonFxAccepted023 from "../../../components/studies/IonFxAccepted023.svelte";
import IonSizeComparison from "../../../components/studies/IonSizeComparison.svelte";
import IonStreamStudy from "../../../components/studies/IonStreamStudy.svelte";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find((entry) => entry.slug === "ion-engines-2026-10-31");
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
	<section aria-labelledby="problem">
		<h2 id="problem">01 / A precise ion visual language</h2>
		<p>
			Ion is the dependable baseline engine: a narrow, strongly directed stream
			and a clear start and stop. This study now focuses on how Light and Heavy
			ion engines express that character through their bodies and effects.
		</p>
		<p>
			The shared effects language uses luminous surfaces, gradients, glow and
			overlap. Complete beam marks accumulate at the nozzle, drift in world
			space, and fade after emission stops. The established ion color and the
			accepted treatment in 02.3 anchor this comparison.
		</p>
		<p>
			The original joint investigation of propulsion and appearance has pivoted
			to visuals only. Flight behavior remains unresolved and outside the
			current scope; the examples use motion only to reveal the effects.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="exploration">
		<h2 id="exploration">02 / The engine in use</h2>
		<h3>02.1 / A narrow stream made of luminous surfaces</h3>
		<p>
			The first candidate concentrates the shared plume into a slender stream: a
			bright center surrounded by soft colored edges, with overlapping surfaces
			that stretch, drift and dissolve. Each nozzle has one directed emission.
			During a turn, earlier exhaust keeps its place in the world while new
			emission follows the engine. On release, fresh emission stops immediately
			and the remaining light completes its fade.
		</p>
		<IonStreamStudy client:load />
		<p>
			The synchronized views isolate one proposal: compress surface width to 24%
			of the current plume, retaining its length, palette, glow and 0.65-second
			lifetime. Look at the stream’s connection to the nozzle, the separation
			between two active engines, and the residue left during the turn. The
			current output-driven sizing is retained, so the stream also contracts as
			acceleration diminishes. These proportions are exploratory.
		</p>
		<p>
			This fixture uses the production Fighter, a Small ion side pair, empty
			optional mounts, real mount geometry, engine impulses, energy costs and
			shared effect lifecycle. It starts at rest with full energy. Gravity,
			charging and arena wrapping are omitted to isolate propulsion; the camera
			and enlarged scale are study fixtures. This comparison examines appearance
			in motion, not final handling, low-power behavior or gameplay-scale
			clarity.
		</p>
		<h3 id="linear">02.2 / Parallel light, not a flare</h3>
		<p>
			The first comparison moved in the right direction, but its expanding,
			tapered silhouette still reads as a plume. The refined direction is closer
			to a lightsaber: a very straight beam with parallel sides, a bright core
			and a compact colored halo. Precision comes from a stable width and clear
			axis, rather than a narrow version of a cone.
		</p>
		<IonStreamStudy linear label="Linear ion beam comparison" client:load />
		<p>
			The earlier compressed plume remains on the left. On the right, a filled
			light surface follows each active nozzle, with a rounded end and the same
			effect palette. Output changes its length while its width stays constant.
			The shared drifting surfaces remain as a faint wake, subordinate to the
			beam instead of building a widening flare. The flight sequence and
			physical conditions are unchanged from 02.1.
		</p>
		<p>
			This version separated active light from residual exhaust: release cuts
			the attached beam immediately while a faint wake finishes fading. Review
			identified that split as contrary to the intended single effects language.
			It is preserved here to make the correction in 02.3 inspectable.
		</p>
		<h3 id="fx">02.3 / The beam itself leaves a fading impression</h3>
		<p>
			Ion’s straight shape belongs inside the shared effects system. Each
			simulation step deposits a complete luminous beam mark at the nozzle.
			Successive marks overlap into the active stream; every mark then moves and
			fades in world space. Releasing the engine stops new marks, leaving the
			whole visible effect to finish fading. There is no separate attached beam
			that disappears on release.
		</p>
		<IonFxAccepted023 fx label="Ion FX lifecycle comparison" client:load />
		<p>
			Both views now include one Small ion aft engine alongside the side pair.
			All three beams keep straight sides and fade to transparency toward their
			ends, including the core and halo. The aft engine uses its real mount,
			thrust, mass and energy draw; it fires with the side pair and cuts out
			during the single-engine turn.
		</p>
		<p>
			Both views use the same updated mark and flight sequence. The first
			retains 02.2’s attached-beam method; the second routes all its light
			through the production effect’s emission history, motion and 0.65-second
			fade. Shape is the study variation. The bright core, colored halo and
			residue now share one lifecycle, accumulating like a luminous impression
			that gradually clears.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Visuals accepted · 31 October 2026.</strong>
			The approved reference is the second view, “02.3 · beam marks in the
			shared FX system,” with one aft ion engine and the side pair. Acceptance
			covers the complete emitted beam marks, established palette, bright core
			and halo, transparent ends, overlapping world-space light and its fade
			after release. The resulting appearance matches the intended visual
			experience, including the spread of earlier emissions. The attached-beam
			comparison is not the selected result.
		</p>
		<p>
			This accepted example is preserved unchanged as the visual reference. Its
			visual reference uses a 0.65-second lifetime, 80% inherited velocity, 45
			units/s backward drift and a quadratic fade. The pictured acceleration,
			speed ceiling, steering, power costs and control coupling are not approved
			mechanics. Visual acceptance is separate from production integration.
		</p>
		<h3 id="sizes">02.4 / Light and Heavy, one visual family</h3>
		<p>
			<strong>Pivot · visuals only · 31 October 2026.</strong>
			The propulsion and movement investigations after 02.3 were withdrawn:
			their growing complexity was not advancing the study. The accepted 02.3
			visuals remain the reference. Thrust, speed ceilings, mass, power and
			handling are deferred.
		</p>
		<p>
			Light keeps the accepted slender beam. Heavy pairs its established deeper
			casing with a longer, slightly broader stream and a subtle cooler
			blue-cyan tint. Light retains its approved mint-cyan. Both share a
			near-white core, one precise axis and a transparent end, emitting complete
			marks into the shared FX layer.
		</p>
		<IonSizeComparison client:load />
		<p>
			<strong>Accepted · 31 October 2026.</strong>
			Light and Heavy beam proportions, their subtle color difference, rotation
			and shared fading FX treatment are approved. Integration is limited to
			these two ion variants.
		</p>
		<p>
			Both Fighters use their real engine bodies and nozzle positions, with a
			side pair and one aft engine. Light is the existing Small ion. Heavy’s
			accepted beam is 1.4× as long and 1.45× as wide. Its colored halo and
			fading marks shift toward blue-cyan; opacity, core color and fade lifetime
			stay unchanged. Both views replay the motion, turn and output history from
			accepted 02.3, preserving how its marks overlap and fade. Heavy follows
			that same reference sequence to isolate appearance, not predict
			performance.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="readiness">
		<h2 id="readiness">03 / Ready for an ion-only visual integration</h2>
		<p>
			<strong>Approved · 31 October 2026.</strong>
			The Light and Heavy comparison in 02.4 is the accepted result, built on
			the emitted beam marks from 02.3. Light retains mint-cyan; Heavy uses the
			cooler blue-cyan tint and a beam 1.4× as long and 1.45× as wide. Both keep
			the near-white core, transparent ends, overlap, world-space drift and
			fade. Rotation must leave earlier emissions behind rather than rotating
			the accumulated effect with the ship.
		</p>
		<p>
			The current Canvas renderer, real nozzle geometry and shared EnginePlume
			lifecycle already support the result. The study supplies the beam painter;
			production still uses the generic plume painter. Integration is feasible
			without new dependencies or a second effects layer.
		</p>
		<p>
			The necessary adaptation is per-emission visual identity. Emitters and
			their stored marks currently carry geometry and output, but no engine
			style. Each new mark must retain its style so mixed loadouts and fading
			residue render correctly. Selecting a painter for the whole ship would
			incorrectly change other engines.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong
				>Scope: only Small ion (Light, <code>ion</code>) and Heavy ion (<code
					>heavy-ion</code
				>).</strong
			>
			Fusion, plasma and untyped legacy exhaust retain their existing appearance
			and behavior. Engine bodies and mounts are already shared and need no
			redesign. Thrust, speed limits, mass, power, controls, gravity and
			friction are outside this integration.
		</p>
		<p>
			The accepted sequence establishes visual fidelity, not Heavy’s handling:
			both views deliberately use Light’s motion and output history. Production
			must retain each engine’s existing output calculation. The earlier failure
			with constant speed and full output shows why the accepted replay must
			remain a comparison reference. Gameplay-scale clarity and mixed-loadout
			fidelity still need inspection during integration; they do not reopen
			propulsion design.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="integration">
		<h2 id="integration">
			04 / Promote the beam marks, preserve other engines
		</h2>
		<p>
			The plan below is now implemented locally. The shared painter is used in
			fitted flight and the <a href="/lab/ion-engines">canonical ion FX Lab</a>,
			which also retains the frozen approved reference. Release verification and
			publication are tracked separately.
		</p>
		<ol>
			<li>
				<strong>Preserve the reference.</strong>
				Keep 02.3 and the approved 02.4 palette, proportions, motion and output
				sequence inspectable. Capture forward flight, rotation and release at
				fixed simulation ticks before switching the live example to shared
				definitions.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Define the two ion styles in core.</strong>
				Promote the study’s filled beam painter and named palette/proportion
				definitions from <code>IonSizeComparison.svelte</code> into the core
				effects module. Retain the existing generic painter as the default.
				Define the style schema/type before extending emitter and stored-mark
				contracts; core must not depend on Mode A’s part catalog.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Assign styles per nozzle.</strong>
				In
				<code>mode-a/lib/build-renderer.ts</code>, map only <code>ion</code> and
				<code>heavy-ion</code>
				to their new styles in <code>buildPlumeEmitters</code>. In
				<code>core/lib/effects/engine-plume.ts</code>, copy that identity into
				each emitted mark and select its painter at draw time. Missing identity,
				fusion and plasma continue through the original painter. Keep the shared
				lifetime, drift, capacity and output sizing intact.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Use the existing game path.</strong>
				Keep Simulation’s emission and FlightDeck’s effects draw connected to
				the same shared system. Update the canonical flight/builder Lab to
				demonstrate Light, Heavy and a mixed ion/non-ion loadout. Preserve
				existing physics and power delivery, including Heavy’s actual output in
				gameplay.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Document and verify.</strong>
				Record the accepted ion-only visual rules in
				<code>docs/design.md</code>
				and the relevant ship-builder specification when delivered. Verify
				per-mark style retention, default fallback and mixed-engine isolation,
				then run repository gates. Mark this study integrated only after
				implementation and fidelity review.
			</li>
		</ol>
		<p>
			Visual review must compare the shared painter against the frozen accepted
			sequence at the same ticks, scale, camera and output: forward thrust, the
			four-second turn, shutdown at 5.25 seconds and the remaining fade. Check
			Light and Heavy separately, then together with fusion or plasma. Non-ion
			frames must match their pre-change baseline; changing a loadout or
			stopping an engine must not recolor marks already emitted.
		</p>
		<p>
			Inspect the result at gameplay zoom on Fighter, Scout and Heavy Corvette,
			including close mounts, low output and release. Any necessary visual
			tuning returns for review against the approved reference. No propulsion
			tuning or changes to other engine families are included.
		</p>
	</section>
</StudyLayout>
